  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA batch of showers and embedded thunderstorms early this morning \nwith continue moving east, and should exit the forecast area by 3-5 \nAM, before sunrise. There could be some patchy fog and lingering low \nclouds around through early to mid morning as well. Otherwise, skies \nlook to become partly to mostly sunny for the late morning and \nafternoon hours. A weak wave then moves through in the northwest \nflow pattern on the northeast side of the ridge; moving from \nsouthern Ontario toward Central PA or portions of NE PA. This could \nkickoff some isolated to scattered showers and t'storms during the \nlate afternoon and evening hours. With lower instability expected, \nstrong or severe storms are not expected at this time for our \nforecast area. Otherwise, dew point will be lower today across \nCentral NY, in the low to mid 60s, so it will feel a bit less humid. \nHumidity remains rather high in NE PA, where dew points hover in the \nmid 60s to lower 70s into the afternoon hours. Temperatures will be \na few degrees lower than yesterday; with highs in the mid 70s to \nlower 80s expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nA more northerly, drier flow develops for Saturday into Sunday over \nthe forecast area. A deep upper level trough will settle in over \nnorthern New England and the Canadian Maritimes over the weekend. \nSome mid and upper level moisture will try to rotate in from the \nnorth-northeast into  our area by Saturday afternoon and evening. \nThis could be enough to initiate some isolated showers; especially \nover the Mohawk Valley and Catskills. Confidence on exact amounts of \ncloud cover and any shower potential remains low. Did bump up PoPs \ninto the 10-20% range to account for a few isolated showers in this \ntimeframe. Otherwise, dew points and temperatures will be much \nlower, with refreshing humidity levels and highs 70 to 75 degrees \nexpected. Northwest winds will be slightly breezy, between about 6 \nto 15 mph. Sunday feature much of the same with north winds \ncontinuing and a bit more in the way of sunshine expected. Cool \ntemperatures continue with highs in the upper 60s to low 70s. High \npressure moves overhead Sunday night into Labor Day. This will bring \ncool overnight lows and widespread valley fog into Monday morning. \nMonday afternoon should feature plenty of sunshine, light winds and \ncomfortable temperatures in the low to mid-70s. Tuesday is expected \nto remain dry and mostly sunny with continued mild temperatures as \nhigh pressure remains in place.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \n\nEarly indications are that an upper level trough will approach and \nmove through the Northeast US for the middle to end of the upcoming \nworkweek. This would bring increasing southerly flow, moisture, \ninstability and warmer temperatures. It also acts to increase \nchances for showers and thunderstorms as the frontal boundary \napproaches and moves through the region. Temperatures will rise into \nthe mid 70s to low 80s, with dew points reaching back into the 60s. \nThe exact timing of the trough and frontal passage remains uncertain \nthis far out in time. The current model ensembles want to dry things \nout and bring cooler/less humid weather to the area by next Friday, \nbut this is 7 days out, so the timing could change.",
